  "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{\u2032}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{\u2032}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{\u2032}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+8)-(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+8)^{\u2032}}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+8)-(x+1)\\cdot (2x)}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+8-(2{x}^{2}+2x)}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}-2x+8}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{\u2032}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-2x+8}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}-2x+8 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-4$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So, $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are \n\n$$\nx=-4\\,\\text{and}\\,x=2.\n$$",
